WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.002
Three sources agree

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:04.628
but they all copied the same original.

00:00:05.967 --> 00:00:12.453
In twenty twenty-three, lawyers in New York filed a brief that cited court opinions that did not exist.

00:00:12.753 --> 00:00:17.681
They had been generated by ChatGPT, complete with quotes and citations.

00:00:17.981 --> 00:00:22.078
The court sanctioned the lawyers and their firm: five thousand dollars.

00:00:22.378 --> 00:00:25.877
A citation that looks right is not the same as support.

00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:29.794
Now a quieter version of the same problem.

00:00:30.094 --> 00:00:35.215
Your policy needs two independent sources, and search returns three that agree.

00:00:35.515 --> 00:00:41.894
Trace them, and all three come from one handbook: a PDF, a web mirror, and a forwarded email.

00:00:42.194 --> 00:00:44.541
Three citations. One witness.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.143
QualityRag counts origins, not citations.

00:00:49.443 --> 00:00:55.609
Three citations from one handbook make one independent source, so the check is inconclusive.

00:00:55.909 --> 00:01:01.051
Add minutes from the change board, a genuinely separate origin, and it admits.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:10.471
The console shows it too: the mirrored copy of the handbook is marked as the same origin, and counted once, not twice.

00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:16.165
Independence is declared in the policy, and counted by origin.

00:01:16.465 --> 00:01:25.084
But the origin comes from your metadata. QualityRag does not yet verify authorship, or catch a copy that hides where it came from.

00:01:25.384 --> 00:01:29.139
Next: old rules, and rules that contradict each other.
